        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>Previous studies have shown that long duration exposure to microgravity causes significant bone loss.  In the absence of gravity, bone mass decreases in the load-bearing regions of the skeleton.  This space condition mimics osteoporosis, a medical condition characterized by brittle bones.  By learning more about the process of bone mineral loss and recovery, researchers hope to be able to implement effective countermeasures in space, and develop more effective treatments for those who suffer from bone disorders.<p>The objectives of this experiment are 1) to determine the preflight lean body mass and muscle strength of the Shuttle crewmembers, 2) to determine the lean body mass and muscle strength of the crew during the postflight period, 3) to relate the changes in muscle strength to changes in lean mass, and 4) to relate changes in muscle strength and lean mass to the degree and duration of protein turnover changes postflight, which was measured for a separate experiment called "Protein Turnover During Space Flight" (Principal Investigator - Ferrando).<P>APPROACH:<br>Dual X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA) scans were obtained pre- and postflight using a Hologic QDR 2000 whole body densitometer.  Bone mineral density data were obtained from scans of the whole body, lumbar spine, proximal femur (hip) and calcaneus (heel).  Whole body and regional measurements of lean tissue mass were also obtained from the whole body scans.<p>Isokinetic dynamometry measurements of the knee, ankle and back (concentric and eccentric measurements) were performed pre- and postflight using a LIDO isokinetic dynamometer. <P>RESULTS:<br>Results of this investigation are not available.
        </div>

